    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced></mrow></msub><mo>&#8652;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mfenced></mrow></msub><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"/><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math></p><p>Consider the above reaction. If $20$g of dinitrogen reacts with $5$g of dihydrogen, then the limiting reagent of the reaction and number of moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> formed respectively are</p>"
